# Line-ending normalization.
#
# gen-sw.mjs hashes the app shell's bytes to derive sw.js's cache name and
# index.html's ?v= asset stamps. Those hashes must be identical on every
# checkout — a developer with core.autocrlf=true (CRLF working tree) and CI
# with autocrlf=false (LF) would otherwise compute different hashes for the
# same commit, and the smoke's serviceWorkerCache / indexHtmlCache gates
# would fail one side or the other. Pin every hashed text file to LF so the
# bytes are deterministic everywhere.
* text=auto
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
VERSION text eol=lf
sw.js text eol=lf

# The Windows launchers go the OTHER way, and must. cmd.exe's block parser is
# line-ending sensitive — an LF-only .cmd is a latent "was unexpected at this
# time." waiting to happen, which is exactly the failure check-launchers.cjs
# exists to prevent. They had no rule at all, so `text=auto` normalised them:
# on Windows they happened to check out CRLF and the gate passed, on Linux
# they checked out LF and it failed. That difference was invisible until the
# suite first ran on a Linux runner, and the same normalisation could have
# committed an LF launcher that shipped broken to Windows users.
#
# Only .cmd. PowerShell and wscript read LF happily, so pinning .ps1/.vbs
# would be churn without a hazard behind it.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.wasm binary
*.png binary
*.gz binary
*.pdf binary
